{% load i18n %}<view xmlns="https://hyperview.org/hyperview" id="preference-panel">
  <!-- No <form> of its own: the HOST supplies the form ancestor, because
       getFormData walks up to the closest one and whatever it finds there is the
       whole POST body. The drawer is the single host now, and it has no fields, so
       it supplies a form holding only the CSRF token. A control with NO form
       ancestor posts a null body and Django rejects it at the CSRF check, which is
       precisely why a <switch> cannot be used here at all. Both controls carry
       their TARGET value in the href, so the server writes absolute state and a
       double tap cannot flip anything back.
       HISTORY, not a live constraint: this partial also sat on Settings, where the
       ancestor was settings-form so a theme tap carried the first name the user had
       just typed. Settings became one deferred form with one Save button and a
       tap-commits control could not stay on it. A future second host owes the same
       form ancestor, for the CSRF reason above. -->
  <text style="preference-label">{% translate "Appearance" %}</text>
  <!-- A pill built from views, because the binary control the design asks for
       cannot be a <switch> here at all: HvSwitch clones the element before it
       triggers, the clone has no parentNode, getFormData walks up to nothing and
       the POST body is null, no csrfmiddlewaretoken, so Django rejects the tap.
       The href names the OPPOSITE theme, so the server still writes absolute
       state. The whole row is the target, not just the pill.
       The knob does NOT animate, and it cannot: Hyperview has no style
       transition and `replace` is a DOM swap, so the thumb moves in one frame
       between the two justifyContent positions. The only motion here for free is
       the pressed row fill under the finger. Making it slide needs a native
       component, a store release and a rule-9 fallback for every older binary.
       The label carries the NAME and the STATE, because accessibilityState is
       unreachable: createProps copies attributes verbatim, so a string can never
       become React Native's {checked: boolean}. accessibilityRole="switch" with
       no state announces "switch" and nothing else, so the role is "button".
       No id on that text: createTestProps is spread LAST and on Android an id
       overwrites accessibilityLabel with the slug. No id on the ROW either, for
       the same reason one step up: HyperRef passes its own TouchableOpacity
       createTestProps (hyper-ref.tsx:299 and 343), which is accessibilityLabel=id
       on every non-iOS platform (services/index.ts:81-84), and a wrapper carrying
       a contentDescription is the node TalkBack focuses, so the row would say
       "theme-toggle" instead of the state on the label below it. Nothing outside
       tests ever read these ids and the replace target is the PANEL, not the
       control, so they select by style or href now. Same rule, same reason, as
       the dashboard hero trigger in tests/test_settings.py. -->
  <view style="pref-toggle-row" href="/hv/preferences/?theme={% if theme_name == 'dark' %}light{% else %}dark{% endif %}" verb="post" action="replace" target="preference-panel">
    <text style="pref-toggle-label" accessibilityRole="button" accessibilityLabel="{% if theme_name == 'dark' %}{% translate "Dark theme, on. Activate to use the light theme." %}{% else %}{% translate "Dark theme, off. Activate to use the dark theme." %}{% endif %}">{% translate "Dark theme" %}</text>
    <view style="pref-toggle-track{% if theme_name == 'dark' %} pref-toggle-track-on{% endif %}"><view style="pref-toggle-knob" /></view>
  </view>
  <text style="preference-label">{% translate "Language" %}</text>
  <view style="preference-row">
    <!-- The option labels are NOT translated: a language is named in its own
         language, because that is the one string a speaker looking for it can
         read. The flag DECORATES that word and never replaces it, which is what
         answers the country-versus-language objection: alone, the stars and
         stripes would tell a British user this app has no English and the
         Spanish flag would exclude every Latin American speaker, but beside the
         word it is only a landmark for the eye. The accessible label belongs to one outer text; its inline
         content uses role="none" so the emoji does not inherit a button role.
         Hyperview does not convert accessibilityElementsHidden to a boolean.
         Keep the emoji without an id and use Android's string-valued
         importantForAccessibility="no". Verify VoiceOver and TalkBack with the
         existing host; and on a ROM whose emoji font lacks flag sequences it
         degrades to two boxed letters next to a word that already carries the
         whole meaning. Emoji rather than PNGs on purpose: a colour flag file
         would have to break the 72x72 monochrome icon convention and grow the
         untinted-glyph allowlist, for a shape both platforms already draw. -->
    <view style="preference-option{% if request.LANGUAGE_CODE == 'en' %} preference-option-current{% endif %}" href="/hv/preferences/?language=en" verb="post" action="replace" target="preference-panel">
      <text style="preference-option-text{% if request.LANGUAGE_CODE == 'en' %} preference-option-text-current{% endif %}" accessibilityRole="button" accessibilityLabel="{% if request.LANGUAGE_CODE == 'en' %}{% translate "English, current language" %}{% else %}{% translate "Switch to English" %}{% endif %}"><text accessibilityRole="none"><text style="preference-option-flag" importantForAccessibility="no">🇬🇧</text>&#160;English</text></text>
    </view>
    <view style="preference-option{% if request.LANGUAGE_CODE == 'es' %} preference-option-current{% endif %}" href="/hv/preferences/?language=es" verb="post" action="replace" target="preference-panel">
      <text style="preference-option-text{% if request.LANGUAGE_CODE == 'es' %} preference-option-text-current{% endif %}" accessibilityRole="button" accessibilityLabel="{% if request.LANGUAGE_CODE == 'es' %}{% translate "Español, current language" %}{% else %}{% translate "Switch to Español" %}{% endif %}"><text accessibilityRole="none"><text style="preference-option-flag" importantForAccessibility="no">🇪🇸</text>&#160;Español</text></text>
    </view>
  </view>
</view>
